A data race occurs when two threads access the same memory location without synchronization. Consequences: corrupted data, crashes, undefined behavior.

Using std::mutex
std::mutex m;
int counter = 0;
void increment() {
for (int i = 0; i < 100000; ++i) {
std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);
++counter;
}
}


std::lock_guard represents a strict lexical ownership model: acquire once, release at scope exit. std::unique_lock represents a movable, stateful lock-ownership object whose 
ownership can be transferred, released, reacquired, and used with condition variables.


std::lock_guard:

#include <mutex>

std::mutex m;
int counter = 0;

void increment()
{
    std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(m);

    ++counter;

} // lock_guard destructor automatically unlocks m



Conceptually:

enter scope
    |
    | lock()
    |
    | critical section
    |
exit scope
    |
    | unlock()
    v


You cannot do:

lock.unlock();   // ❌
lock.lock();     // ❌

The lock lifetime is tied directly to the scope.


std::unique_lock-

#include <mutex>

std::mutex m;
int counter = 0;

void increment()
{
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m);

    ++counter;

} // unlock automatically


At first glance it looks identical.
But unique_lock can do much more:

void example()
{
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m);

    // Critical section

    lock.unlock();

    // Non-critical work

    lock.lock();

    // Critical section again
}


So its state can be:

unique_lock
    |
    +-- owns mutex
    |
    +-- does not own mutex
            |
            +-- lock() --> owns



| Feature                                                                  | `std::lock_guard` | `std::unique_lock` |
|---                                                                       |      ---          |                 ---|
| RAII                                                                     | Yes               | Yes                |
| Automatically unlocks                                                    | Yes               | Yes                |
| `lock()` manually                                                        | No                | Yes                |
| `unlock()` manually                                                      | No                | Yes                |
| Can defer locking                                                        | No                | Yes                |
| Can try-lock                                                             | No                | Yes                |
| Can transfer ownership                                                   | No                | Yes                |
| Works with `condition_variable`                                          | No                | **Yes**            |
| Movable                                                                  | No                | **Yes**            |
| Copyable                                                                 | No                | No                 |
| Complexity                                                               | Lower             | Higher             |
| Typical overhead                                                         | Minimal           | Slightly more state/logic |
| Best use                                                                 | Simple critical section | Complex lock lifetime |



Why unique_lock is necessary for condition_variable-

Consider a producer-consumer queue.

#include <condition_variable>
#include <mutex>
#include <queue>

std::mutex m;
std::condition_variable cv;
std::queue<int> q;

void consumer()
{
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(m);

    cv.wait(lock, [] {
        return !q.empty();
    });

    int value = q.front();
    q.pop();
}

The interesting part is:

cv.wait(lock, ...);

condition_variable::wait() needs to:
1. Atomically release the mutex.
2. Put the thread to sleep.
3. Wake the thread.
4. Reacquire the mutex.
5. Return to the caller.


Conceptually:

